Document: Practice Direction 47 – Procedure for Detailed Assessment of Costs and Default Provisions
Part 3 of 5
==================================================
The request must be accompanied by—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (a) a copy of the notice of commencement of detailed assessment proceedings;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (b) a copy of the bill of costs,

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (c) the document giving the right to detailed assessment (see paragraph 13.3 below);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (d) a copy of the points of dispute, annotated as necessary in order to show which items have been agreed and their value and to show which items remain in dispute and their value;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (e) as many copies of the points of dispute so annotated as there are persons who have served points of dispute;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (f) a copy of any replies served;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (g) copies of all orders made by the court relating to the costs which are to be assessed;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (h) copies of the fee notes and other written evidence as served on the paying party in accordance with paragraph 5.2 above;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (i) where there is a dispute as to the receiving party’s liability to pay costs to the legal representatives who acted for the receiving party, any agreement, letter or other written information provided by the legal representative to the client explaining how the legal representative’s charges are to be calculated;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (j) a statement signed by the receiving party or the legal representative giving the name, email address, address for service, reference and telephone number and fax number, if any, of—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (i) the receiving party;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (ii) the paying party;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (iii) any other person who has served points of dispute or who has given notice to the receiving party under paragraph 5.5(1)(b) above;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] and giving an estimate of the length of time the detailed assessment hearing will take;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (k) where the application for a detailed assessment hearing is made by a party other than the receiving party, such of the documents set out in this paragraph as are in the possession of that party;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (l) where the court is to assess the costs of an assisted person or LSC funded client or person to whom civil legal services (within the meaning of Part 1 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012) are provided under arrangement made for the purposes of that Part of that Act—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (i) the legal aid certificate, LSC certificate, the certificate recording the determination of the Director of Legal Aid Casework and relevant amendment certificates, any authorities and any certificates of discharge or revocation or withdrawal;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (ii) a certificate, in Precedent F(3) of the Schedule of Costs Precedents;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (iii) if that person has a financial interest in the detailed assessment hearing and wishes to attend, the postal address of that person to which the court will send notice of any hearing;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (iv) if the rates payable out of the LSC fund or by the Lord Chancellor under Part 1 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012 are prescribed rates, a schedule to the bill of costs setting out all the items in the bill which are claimed against other parties calculated at the legal aid prescribed rates with or without any claim for enhancement: (further information as to this schedule is set out in paragraph 17 of this Practice Direction);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (v) a copy of any default costs certificate in respect of costs claimed in the bill of costs;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (m) if a costs management order has been made, a breakdown of the costs claimed for each phase of the proceedings. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] 13.3 ‘The document giving the right to detailed assessment’ means such one or more of the following documents as are appropriate to the detailed assessment proceedings—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (a) a copy of the judgment or order of the court or tribunal giving the right to detailed assessment;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (b) a copy of the notice sent by the court under Practice Direction 3B paragraph 1, being notification that a claim has been struck out under rule 3.7 or rule 3.7A1 for non-payment of a fee;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (c) a copy of the notice of acceptance where an offer to settle is accepted under Part 36 (Offers to settle);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (d) a copy of the notice of discontinuance in a case which is discontinued under Part 38 (Discontinuance);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (e) a copy of the award made on an arbitration under any Act or pursuant to an agreement, where no court has made an order for the enforcement of the award;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] (f) a copy of the order, award or determination of a statutorily constituted tribunal or body. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] 13.4 On receipt of the request for a detailed assessment hearing the court will fix a date for the hearing, or, if the costs officer so decides, will give directions or fix a date for a preliminary appointment. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] 13.5 Unless the court otherwise orders, if the only dispute between the parties concerns disbursements, the hearing shall take place in the absence of the parties on the basis of the documents and the court will issue its decision in writing. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] 13.6 The court will give at least 14 days’ notice of the time and place of the detailed assessment hearing to every person named in the statement referred to in paragraph 13.2(j) above. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Detailed assessment hearing: rule 47.14] 13.7 If either party wishes to make an application in the detailed assessment proceedings the provisions of Part 23 apply. ## 13.8

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (1) This paragraph deals with the procedure to be adopted where a date has been given by the court for a detailed assessment hearing and—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (a) the detailed assessment proceedings are settled; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (b) a party to the detailed assessment proceedings wishes to apply to vary the date which the court has fixed; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (c) the parties to the detailed assessment proceedings agree about changes they wish to make to any direction given for the management of the detailed assessment proceedings. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (2) If detailed assessment proceedings are settled, the receiving party must give notice of that fact to the court immediately, preferably by fax. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (3) A party who wishes to apply to vary a direction must do so in accordance with Part 23. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (4) If the parties agree about changes they wish to make to any direction given for the management of the detailed assessment proceedings—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (a) they must apply to the court for an order by consent; and

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (b) they must file a draft of the directions sought and an agreed statement of the reasons why the variation is sought; and

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.8] (c) the court may make an order in the agreed terms or in other terms without a hearing, but it may direct that a hearing is to be listed. ## 13.10

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (1) If a party wishes to vary that party’s bill of costs, points of dispute or a reply, an amended or supplementary document must be filed with the court and copies of it must be served on all other relevant parties. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (2) Permission is not required to vary a bill of costs, points of dispute or a reply but the court may disallow the variation or permit it only upon conditions, including conditions as to the payment of any costs caused or wasted by the variation. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] 13.11 Unless the court directs otherwise the receiving party must file with the court the papers in support of the bill not less than 7 days before the date for the detailed assessment hearing and not more than 14 days before that date. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] 13.12 The papers to be filed in support of the bill and the order in which they are to be arranged are as follows—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (i) instructions and briefs to counsel arranged in chronological order together with all advices, opinions and drafts received and response to such instructions;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (ii) reports and opinions of medical and other experts;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (iii) any other relevant papers;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (iv) a full set of any relevant statements of case

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] (v) correspondence, file notes and attendance notes;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] 13.13 The court may direct the receiving party to produce any document which in the opinion of the court is necessary to enable it to reach its decision. These documents will in the first instance be produced to the court, but the court may ask the receiving party to elect whether to disclose the particular document to the paying party in order to rely on the contents of the document, or whether to decline disclosure and instead rely on other evidence. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 13.10] 13.14 Once the detailed assessment hearing has ended it is the responsibility of the receiving party to remove the papers filed in support of the bill. ## Provisional assessment: rule 47.15

## 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] 14.2 The following provisions of Part 47 and this Practice Direction will apply to cases falling within rule 47.15—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (1) rules 47.1, 47.2, 47.4 to 47.13, 47.14 (except paragraphs (6) and (7)), 47.16, 47.17, 47.20 and 47.21; and

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (2) paragraphs 1, 2, 4 to 12, 13 (with the exception of paragraphs 13.4 to 13.7, 13.9, 13.11 and 13.14), 15, and 16, of this Practice Direction. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] 14.3 In cases falling within rule 47.15, when the receiving party files a request for a detailed assessment hearing, that party must file—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (a) the request in Form N258 (opens in a new tab) ;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (b) the documents set out at paragraphs 8.3 and 13.2 of this Practice Direction;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (c) an additional copy of any paper bill and a statement of the costs, including a statement of the costs claimed in respect of the detailed assessment drawn on the assumption that there will not be a hearing following the provisional assessment;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (d) the offers made (those marked ‘without prejudice save as to costs’ or made under Part 36 must be contained in a sealed envelope, marked ‘Part 36 or similar offers’, but not indicating which party or parties have made them);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.1 The amount of costs referred to in rule 47.15(1) is £75,000.] (e) completed Precedent G (points of dispute and any reply). ## 14.4

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.4] (1) On receipt of the request for detailed assessment and the supporting papers, the court will use its best endeavours to undertake a provisional assessment within 6 weeks.No party will be permitted to attend the provisional assessment. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.4] (2) Once the provisional assessment has been carried out the court will return Precedent G (the points of dispute and any reply) with the court’s provisional assessment noted upon it.Within 14 days of receipt of Precedent G the parties must agree the total sum due to the receiving party on the basis of the court’s provisional assessment.If the parties are unable to agree the arithmetic, they must refer the dispute back to the court for a decision on the basis of written submissions. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.4] 14.5 When considering whether to depart from the order indicated by rule 47.15(10) the court will take into account the conduct of the parties and any offers made. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 14.4] 14.6 If a party wishes to be heard only as to the order made in respect of the costs of the initial provisional assessment, the court will invite each side to make written submissions and the matter will be finally determined without a hearing.The court will decide what if any order for costs to make in respect of this procedure. ## Power to issue an interim certificate: rule 47.16

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Power to issue an interim certificate: rule 47.16] 15 A party wishing to apply for an interim certificate may do so by making an application in accordance with Part 23. ## Final costs certificate: rule 47.17

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Final costs certificate: rule 47.17] 16.1 At the detailed assessment hearing the court will indicate any disallowance or reduction in the sums claimed in the bill of costs by making an appropriate note on the bill. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Final costs certificate: rule 47.17] 16.2 The receiving party must, in order to complete the bill after the detailed assessment hearing make clear the correct figures agreed or allowed in respect of each item and must re-calculate the summary of the bill appropriately. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Final costs certificate: rule 47.17] 16.3 The completed bill of costs must be filed with the court no later than 14 days after the detailed assessment hearing. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Final costs certificate: rule 47.17] 16.4 At the same time as filing the completed bill of costs, the party whose bill it is must also produce receipted fee notes and receipted accounts in respect of all disbursements except those covered by a certificate in Precedent F(5) in the Schedule of Costs Precedents annexed to this Practice Direction. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Final costs certificate: rule 47.17] 16.5 No final costs certificate will be issued until all relevant court fees payable on the assessment of costs have been paid. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Final costs certificate: rule 47.17] 16.6 If the receiving party fails to file a completed bill in accordance with rule 47.17 the paying party may make an application under Part 23 seeking an appropriate order under rule 3.1. ## 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] (a) the amount of any costs which have been agreed between the parties or which have been allowed on detailed assessment;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] (b) where applicable the amount agreed or allowed in respect of VAT on such costs. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] This provision is subject to any contrary statutory provision relating to costs payable out of the Community Legal Service Fund or by the Lord Chancellor under Part 1 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] 16.8 A final costs certificate will include disbursements in respect of the fees of counsel only if receipted fee notes or accounts in respect of those disbursements have been produced to the court and only to the extent indicated by those receipts. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] 16.9 Where the certificate relates to costs payable between parties a separate certificate will be issued for each party entitled to costs. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] 16.10 Form N257 is a model form of interim costs certificate and Form N256 (opens in a new tab) is a model form of final costs certificate. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] 16.11 An application for an order staying enforcement of an interim costs certificate or final costs certificate may be made either—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] (a) to a costs judge or District Judge of the court office which issued the certificate; or

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] (b) to the court (if different) which has general jurisdiction to enforce the certificate. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] 16.12 An interim or final costs certificate may be enforced as if it were a judgment for the payment of an amount of money.However, proceedings for the enforcement of interim costs certificates or final costs certificates may not be issued in the Costs Office. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 16.7 A final costs certificate will show—] 17.1 The time for requesting a detailed assessment under rule 47.18 is within 3 months after the date when the right to detailed assessment arose. ## 17.2

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (1) The request for a detailed assessment of costs must be in Form N258A (opens in a new tab) . The request must be accompanied by—

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (a) a copy of the bill of costs;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (b) the document giving the right to detailed assessment (see paragraph 13.3 above);

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (c) copies of all orders made by the court relating to the costs which are to be assessed;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (d) copies of any fee notes of counsel and any expert in respect of fees claimed in the bill;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (e) written evidence as to any other disbursement which is claimed and which exceeds £500;

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (f) the legal aid certificates, LSC certificates, certificates recording the determinations of the Director of Legal Aid Casework, any relevant amendment certificates, any authorities and any certificates of discharge, revocation or withdrawal; and

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (g) a statement signed by the legal representative giving the representative’s name, address for service, reference, telephone number, e-mail address and, if the assisted person has a financial interest in the detailed assessment and wishes to attend, giving the postal address of that person, to which the court will send notice of any hearing. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] (2) The relevant papers in support of the bill as described in paragraph 13.12 must only be lodged if requested by the costs officer. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] 17.3 Where the court has provisionally assessed a bill of costs it will send to the legal representative a notice, in Form N253 annexed to this practice direction, of the amount of costs which the court proposes to allow together with the bill itself. The legal representative should, if the provisional assessment is to be accepted, then complete the bill. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] 17.4 If the solicitor whose bill it is, or any other party wishes to make an application in the detailed assessment proceedings, the provisions of Part 23 applies. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > 17.2] 17.5 It is the responsibility of the legal representative to complete the bill by entering in the bill the correct figures allowed in respect of each item, recalculating the summary of the bill appropriately and completing the Community Legal Service assessment certificate (Form EX80A (opens in a new tab) ). ## Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates

[PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 17.6 Where the costs of an assisted person or LSC funded client or person to whom civil legal services (within the meaning of Part 1 of the Legal Aid, Sentencing and Punishment of Offenders Act 2012) are provided under arrangements made for the purposes of that Part of that Act are payable by another person but costs can be claimed against the LSC or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates (with or without enhancement), the solicitor of the assisted person or LSC funded client or person to whom civil legal services are provided must file a legal aid/ LSC schedule in accordance with paragraph 13.2(l) above.If on paper (a “paper schedule”) the schedule should follow as closely as possible Precedent E of the Schedule of Costs Precedents annexed to this Practice Direction. If an electronic bill of costs is served on the other person an electronic schedule may, subject to paragraphs 17.7 and 17.8 below, be prepared and filed as if it were an electronic bill. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 17.7 The schedule must set out by reference to the item numbers in the bill of costs, all the costs claimed as payable by another person, but the arithmetic in the schedule should claim those items at prescribed rates only (with or without any claim for enhancement). [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 17.8 Where there has been a change in the prescribed rates during the period covered by the bill of costs, a paper schedule (as opposed to the bill) should be divided into separate parts, so as to deal separately with each change of rate. The paper schedule must also be divided so as to correspond with any divisions in the bill of costs. If the schedule is an electronic schedule, unless the format of the schedule already provides the requisite information it should incorporate a summary in a form comparable to the “Funding and Parts Table” in Precedent S to provide the information that would otherwise be provided by its division into parts. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 17.9 If the bill of costs sets costs claimed against the LSC or Lord Chancellor only, the schedule may be set out in a separate document or, alternatively, may be included in the bill, shown separately from the costs claimed against other parties.. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 17.10 The detailed assessment of the legal aid/ LSC schedule will take place immediately after the detailed assessment of the bill of costs but on occasions, the court may decide to conduct the detailed assessment of the legal aid/ LSC schedule separately from any detailed assessment of the bill of costs. This will occur, for example, where a default costs certificate is obtained as between the parties but that certificate is not set aside at the time of the detailed assessment of the legal aid costs. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 17.11 Where costs have been assessed at prescribed rates it is the responsibility of the legal representative to enter the correct figures allowed in respect of each item and to recalculate the summary of the legal aid/ LSC schedule. [PRACTICE DIRECTION 47 – PROCEDURE FOR DETAILED ASSESSMENT OF COSTS AND DEFAULT PROVISIONS > Costs payable by the legal services commission or Lord Chancellor at prescribed rates] 18.1 Rule 47.19 enables the court to direct under rule 47.19(3) that the receiving party must serve a copy of the request for assessment and copies of the documents which accompany it, on any person who has a financial interest in the outcome of the assessment.
